INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A fabric mill''s technical team explains that ''Momie'' or ''crepe weave'' fabric structure (an irregular, small-repeat interlacing pattern using standard-twist yarn, one of the THREE crepe mechanisms discussed earlier) achieves its textured surface through a fundamentally different principle than EITHER high-twist crepe yarn OR chemical crepe finish. What is this structural mechanism?', '["Momie weave achieves texture through mathematically identical means to high-twist crepe yarn, making any distinction between these two crepe mechanisms technically incorrect","Momie weave uses a deliberately IRREGULAR, small, non-repeating (or very large-repeat) interlacing PATTERN — unlike a regular twill or satin''s systematic, predictable float pattern — this irregularity in WHERE each thread interlaces creates small, randomly-distributed surface bumps/texture directly from the weave geometry itself, using completely ORDINARY, standard-twist yarn — the texture comes entirely from pattern irregularity, not from any special yarn twist property or chemical treatment","Momie weave can only be produced using high-twist crepe yarn, making it functionally identical to (rather than a separate, distinct mechanism from) the yarn-twist-based crepe method already discussed","Crepe weave structure has no relationship to interlacing pattern irregularity, making this entire described structural mechanism technically unfounded"]'::jsonb, 'Momie weave uses a deliberately IRREGULAR, small, non-repeating (or very large-repeat) interlacing PATTERN — unlike a regular twill or satin''s systematic, predictable float pattern — this irregularity in WHERE each thread interlaces creates small, randomly-distributed surface bumps/texture directly from the weave geometry itself, using completely ORDINARY, standard-twist yarn — the texture comes entirely from pattern irregularity, not from any special yarn twist property or chemical treatment', 'This completes the earlier three-mechanism crepe texture discussion (high-twist yarn, chemical finish, and now weave structure) with the specific structural principle behind momie/crepe weave — unlike twill or satin''s REGULAR, predictable, repeating float pattern, momie weave deliberately uses an IRREGULAR interlacing pattern (each thread interlacing at seemingly random, non-systematic points) — this positional irregularity in the weave geometry itself creates small, randomly-distributed surface texture using completely ORDINARY yarn (no special high-twist yarn or chemical treatment needed), representing a genuinely third, structurally distinct mechanism for achieving crepe-type surface texture, completing the full picture alongside the earlier-discussed yarn-twist and chemical-finish mechanisms.', 3, '{"momie-crepe-weave-mechanism"}'::text[]),
('22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A fabric technologist explains ''pique weave'' (a woven structure, distinct from the earlier-discussed KNIT pique) achieves a similar honeycomb-textured surface appearance through WOVEN construction using TWO warp systems (a ground warp and a stitching/binding warp) rather than knitting''s tuck-stitch mechanism. Why does woven pique specifically require this SECOND warp system rather than achieving the texture through weft alone?', '["Woven pique requires no special construction at all and is structurally identical to any basic plain weave fabric, making this described two-warp-system requirement technically unfounded","The raised, cellular texture requires certain WARP threads to be pulled DOWN/held BACK at specific points (creating the recessed ''cell'' areas) while OTHER warp threads remain more free/raised (creating the raised ridge areas) — achieving this differential warp behavior requires a SEPARATE binding/stitching warp system specifically dedicated to creating this pull-down anchoring effect at defined points, since a single warp system alone cannot simultaneously be both ''pulled down'' and ''raised'' at different points within the same structure — this parallels the earlier double-cloth discussion''s principle of using multiple yarn systems to achieve structural effects a single system cannot replicate","Woven pique achieves its texture entirely through the WEFT yarn''s twist level, with zero involvement of any warp system, making the described two-warp-system explanation technically incorrect","Pique weave can only be produced using a single warp system, making the premise of this question about requiring TWO warp systems factually incorrect"]'::jsonb, 'The raised, cellular texture requires certain WARP threads to be pulled DOWN/held BACK at specific points (creating the recessed ''cell'' areas) while OTHER warp threads remain more free/raised (creating the raised ridge areas) — achieving this differential warp behavior requires a SEPARATE binding/stitching warp system specifically dedicated to creating this pull-down anchoring effect at defined points, since a single warp system alone cannot simultaneously be both ''pulled down'' and ''raised'' at different points within the same structure — this parallels the earlier double-cloth discussion''s principle of using multiple yarn systems to achieve structural effects a single system cannot replicate', 'This connects the earlier double-cloth multi-warp-system discussion to a different specific application — woven pique''s characteristic raised, cellular texture requires certain warp threads to be actively ANCHORED DOWN at specific points (creating recessed cells) while adjacent areas remain relatively free/raised (creating the ridge/cell texture) — since a SINGLE warp system''s threads all behave according to the same basic weave rule throughout, achieving this genuinely DIFFERENTIAL behavior (some points anchored down, others free) requires a dedicated SECOND (binding/stitching) warp system specifically for creating this anchoring effect at defined points — directly paralleling the earlier double-cloth principle that certain structural effects genuinely require multiple independent yarn systems working together, which a single system cannot replicate regardless of how it''s woven.', 3, '{"woven-pique-two-warp-mechanism"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
